---
layout: course
title: Introduction to Pattern Recognition
code: CSE 455/555
level: Undergraduate and graduate
taught: Summers, 2023–2026
prereq: A programming or data-structures course (CSE 115 or 250, EAS 230 or 240) and probability or statistics (EAS 305, STA 301, or MTH 411)
text: Landmark research papers
grading: Absolute scale, no curve
description: CSE 455/555 Introduction to Pattern Recognition at the University at Buffalo — taught through the papers that shaped the field.
lede: >-
  Pattern recognition taught through the research papers that shaped it — from the first deep vision networks to the
  Transformer and today's large language models.
about: >-
  <p>Rather than learning only from lectures and problem sets, students go straight to the original papers. Each session
  pairs a landmark paper with a short framing lecture and discussion, and students turn the key ideas into working code
  in Python. By the end of the term, you know the central techniques of the field and, just as importantly, how to read a
  research paper critically.</p>
semesters:
  - value: summer-2026
    label: Summer 2026
    selected: true
    intro: >-
      A paper-reading seminar: twelve sessions over six weeks, each opening with a short quiz on that day's paper. No exams;
      the rest of the term goes into the final project.
    assessment:
      - [In-class quizzes, 50%]
      - [Participation, 15%]
      - [Final project, 35%]
    outline:
      - group: Vision foundations
      - when: Session 1
        topic: >-
          How to read a paper — <a href="https://web.stanford.edu/class/ee384m/Handouts/HowtoReadPaper.pdf" rel="noopener">Keshav, 2007</a>
        guide: The three-pass reading method used all term.
      - when: Session 2
        topic: >-
          <a href="https://proceedings.neurips.cc/paper/4824-imagenet-classification-with-deep-convolutional-neural-networks.pdf" rel="noopener">AlexNet</a> (Krizhevsky et al., 2012)
        guide: The result that launched the deep-learning era, and which of its ingredients still matter.
      - when: Session 3
        topic: >-
          <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1512.03385" rel="noopener">ResNet</a> (He et al., 2015)
        guide: Why skip connections make networks hundreds of layers deep trainable.
      - group: Sequences and the Transformer
      - when: Session 4
        topic: >-
          <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1301.3781" rel="noopener">Word2Vec</a> (Mikolov et al., 2013)
        guide: How words become vectors, learned without labels.
      - when: Session 5
        topic: >-
          <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1409.3215" rel="noopener">Sequence to sequence</a> (Sutskever et al., 2014) and
          <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1409.0473" rel="noopener">attention</a> (Bahdanau et al., 2015)
        guide: From a single fixed context vector to attention over every input.
      - when: Session 6
        topic: >-
          <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.03762" rel="noopener">Attention Is All You Need</a> (Vaswani et al., 2017)
        guide: The Transformer, with recurrence removed entirely.
      - group: The large-language-model era
      - when: Session 7
        topic: >-
          <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1810.04805" rel="noopener">BERT</a> (Devlin et al., 2018)
        guide: Pretraining and fine-tuning on the encoder side.
      - when: Session 8
        topic: >-
          <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.14165" rel="noopener">GPT-3</a> (Brown et al., 2020)
        guide: Scale and in-context learning on the decoder side.
      - when: Session 9
        topic: >-
          <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2001.08361" rel="noopener">Scaling laws</a> (Kaplan et al., 2020) and
          <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.15556" rel="noopener">Chinchilla</a> (Hoffmann et al., 2022)
        guide: How to split a compute budget between model size and training data.
      - when: Session 10
        topic: >-
          <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2203.02155" rel="noopener">InstructGPT and RLHF</a> (Ouyang et al., 2022)
        guide: Turning a raw language model into one that follows instructions.
      - when: Session 11
        topic: >-
          <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2201.11903" rel="noopener">Chain-of-thought prompting</a> and
          <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2206.07682" rel="noopener">emergent abilities</a> (Wei et al., 2022)
        guide: Reasoning through prompting, and abilities that appear with scale.
      - when: Session 12
        topic: >-
          Reasoning models — <a href="https://openai.com/index/learning-to-reason-with-llms/" rel="noopener">OpenAI o1</a> (2024) and
          <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.12948" rel="noopener">DeepSeek-R1</a> (2025); final-project talks
        guide: Test-time compute and reasoning trained with reinforcement learning.
  - value: summer-2025
    label: Summer 2025
    intro: >-
      Two papers per session, from neural-network foundations through vision and language to graphs and robustness.
    assessment:
      - [Attendance, 15%]
      - [Exam 1, 35%]
      - [Exam 2, 25%]
      - [Project, 25%]
    outline:
      - when: Week 1
        topic: Back-propagation, dropout, ReLU, and Adam
      - when: Week 2
        topic: Batch normalization, the RNN encoder–decoder, AlexNet, and VGG
      - when: Week 3
        topic: ResNet, Inception, U-Net, and Faster R-CNN
      - when: Week 4
        topic: YOLO, Mask R-CNN, EfficientNet, and the Transformer
      - when: Week 5
        topic: BERT, GPT-2, RoBERTa, and T5
      - when: Week 6
        topic: Graph attention networks, Deep Graph Infomax, and adversarial robustness
      - when: Week 7
        topic: Course wrap-up and future directions
---
<div data-semester-year="summer-2026">
  <h2>Final project</h2>
  <p>
    One capstone for everyone: <strong>build a small language model from scratch, post-train it to follow instructions,
    and investigate a sharp question about reasoning and scale</strong>, drawing on the whole second half of the course.
    It is graded as a short research paper across six milestones, from proposal to final talk, with required baselines,
    ablations, and reproducible code.
  </p>
</div>
